---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InterestForm from "../components/InterestForm.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Digital nomad residence — D.3"
  description="Andorra D.3 digital nomad residence: quota, 90 days, economy-ministry resolution, means set by regulation."
  path="/digital-nomad"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Digital nomad residence — D.3</h1>
    <p>
      The Government still publishes a dedicated D.3 page. What follows
      is that page, in English.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Source:
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-per-a-nomada-digital"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          >D.3. Residència per a nòmada digital — Govern d’Andorra</a
        >.
        Financial means are “those set by regulation”. If the official
        page changes, use that page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What the Government says D.3 is</h2>
    <p>
      D.3 authorises the holder to reside in Andorra for the life of the
      authorisation. It may be requested, under current law, by a
      newcomer whose work does not require a fixed geographic location
      and who uses telecommunications and technology, and who:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>comes within the quota then set by regulation;</li>
      <li>
        establishes principal and effective residence in Andorra for at
        least 90 days per calendar year (the figure is on the official
        page).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How long the official page says it lasts</h2>
    <p>
      The initial residence authorisation is granted for two years; the
      first renewal for two years; the second for three years; later
      renewals for ten years, except for nationals of states that have
      signed and ratified a convention with Andorra. That is the
      Government’s wording on 29 August 2026. Confirm it on the live page.
    </p>

    <h2>Documents listed (non-exhaustive)</h2>
    <p>
      The official list is expressly “enunciative and not limiting”. It
      includes, among other items:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>the completed residence-without-work application;</li>
      <li>passport, or for EU/EEA nationals a national identity card;</li>
      <li>
        criminal-record certificates from the country of origin, of
        nationality and of residence, plus a sworn declaration;
      </li>
      <li>a recent colour photograph to the stated format;</li>
      <li>
        a favourable resolution from the ministry responsible for the
        economy qualifying the person as someone whose work needs no
        fixed location and uses telecommunications and technology;
      </li>
      <li>
        evidence of owning or renting a habitable dwelling, or of having
        started a purchase that must complete within one year of the
        residence application;
      </li>
      <li>civil-status documents as listed on the page;</li>
      <li>
        a written commitment to the 90-day presence and to keeping
        insurance in force;
      </li>
      <li>
        sufficient economic means as set by regulation, for the
        applicant and dependants;
      </li>
      <li>
        illness and incapacity cover for Andorra for the life of the
        residence (the page states a narrower illness-only cover for
        minors and for people over sixty);
      </li>
      <li>
        Comú registration within one month of the grant, and
        medical-review papers.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Foreign official documents must carry a Hague apostille or be
      otherwise legalised. That sentence is on the same page.
    </p>
